  ],
  "text": "A star is a permanent argument between gravity pulling in and fusion pushing out. Gravity wants to crush it to a point. Fusion in the core pushes back exactly hard enough. That standoff is what a star is, and it lasts precisely as long as the fuel does. The Sun has held it for about four and a half billion years, with roughly the same left. Here is the part that surprises people. Bigger stars die sooner. More mass means more crushing, so it has to burn far harder just to hold itself up. The largest stars burn through everything in a few million years. Small red dwarfs last trillions. Which means essentially every small star ever formed is still burning right now. When fusion finally loses, a small star swells up and then quietly collapses into a white dwarf. The big ones lose the argument all at once, and the explosion makes everything you are made of. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
